costs ~2.4 s per exponent on the Electron/BoringSSL runtime this fork ships (an unauthenticated public request stalling the main thread), reusing one object retains the last secret exponent, and Electron's BoringSSL rejects the `KeyObject` import that would have avoided the constructor; the residual per-limb `BigInt` timing variation is stated rather than defended — `x` is computed locally at window open with no request in flight, and a fresh `b` is used for exactly two exponentiations before being dropped; `A` is refused unless it decodes to EXACTLY 384 canonical base64url bytes with `1 < A < N-1` and is never reduced mod `N`; that range check runs BEFORE the derivation is charged rather than inside the arithmetic, because a degenerate `A` is a free-to-forge constant (`PAD(0)`, `PAD(1)`, `PAD(N-1)`) and charging it first let a few dozen such packets exhaust the window's 64 new derivations without the desktop ever performing one — the order is validate, charge, derive; `u == 0` is refused; all of it fails as `pair_bad_key_agreement` rather than as a wrong code. Everything is domain-separated from the QR flow (salt `superset-companion/pair/v3-srp`, labels `sc/v3-srp device `/`confirm-phone`/`confirm-desktop`, an 825-byte `sc/v3-srp` transcript binding `pid ‖ deviceId ‖ PAD(A) ‖ PAD(B) ‖ pairSalt` — deliberately NOT RFC 2945's `M1`/`M2`, which would leave `pid` and `deviceId` unauthenticated — and `/v1/pair/confirm` bound into the step-4 AAD so a captured envelope cannot be replayed across flows), while the QR/LAN flow's wire bytes are unchanged BYTE FOR BYTE with pinned goldens proving it, and two cross-runtime differential vector cases (one with a leading-zero code) are frozen jointly with the Android client after both sides derived them independently — TypeScript/OpenSSL here, Java/BouncyCastle there — and agreed on every value. Grinding is ONLINE-only and bounded by, in order, the endpoint answering a bodyless 404 (byte-identical to an unknown path) unless a window is open, ONE window at a time process-wide across BOTH kinds, opened only by the person at the desktop, 120 s, single use, three bad MACs burning it, five confirms per window, a per-source request cap keyed on `cf-connecting-ip`, per-candidate ownership by that source, a MONOTONE per-window ceiling on TOTAL admitted requests (32x32, charged before the body is read, never refunded — the per-source table evicts its oldest bucket to keep the real phone reachable, which refunds that source, so the aggregate bound has to be stated separately to be true; the per-source rule is charged FIRST and the aggregate only once it passes, because charging the aggregate first let ONE address spend the whole window total on requests it was simultaneously being refused as a flood, locking out the phone the per-source rule exists to protect) and a MONOTONE ceiling of 64 genuinely-new key derivations per window (the candidate table evicts rather than refuses, so nothing else bounds how many exponentiations a flood can buy; a cached exact retry, a refused rewrite, a `begin` flood and every `confirm` cost nothing against it) — at most three guesses out of 10^8 per window a human deliberately opened. Reachability is HOST-scoped and fails closed: the three pairing paths are served ONLY on the dedicated public host `pair-superset.khaira.family` and by POST only (anything else there, trailing slash included, is a bare 404), those same paths are a bare 404 on the main host, an unknown or missing `Host` reaches nothing, and every ordinary route keeps its unchanged main Cloudflare Access JWT validation — the host/method/path gate is registered BEFORE the Access gate, which is what lets a phone with no Access token pair at all. There is no pairing-scoped Access token any more: one that ships inside a published APK is public by construction and was never a security boundary, so the PAKE is the boundary and the residual exposure — an unauthenticated public endpoint that a distributed flood can make expensive — is accepted and documented rather than papered over. Absent `~/.superset/companion/public-pair-host.json` the code flow is simply off (the QR flow is untouched) and `openRemotePairing` refuses with a message naming the file to create; a file that is present but malformed — or that names the MAIN application host — throws at start-up rather than silently falling back. A burned window is remembered for 60 s so the phone can be told "you burned it" instead of the bare 404 a never-opened window produces. Because that candidate table EVICTS oldest-first rather than refusing a newcomer, a phone can lose the candidate its own `begin` created — a flood arriving inside its `begin` -> `kex` gap displaces it while the window stays open and usable — so both remote steps answer `pair_unknown_candidate` there instead of the QR flow's `pair_wrong_peer`: the true advice is "repeat begin", and "something else answered instead of the desktop" reads as an attack for a case where nothing substituted itself. The missing candidate is itself the proof that `begin` must be repeated, so nothing is tombstoned to remember it. It is returned from `/v1/pair/kex` AND `/v1/pair/confirm` (eviction can land in either gap) and ONLY after the window, wire version, source and `pid` have all checked out — a wrong `pid`, a second source driving someone else's candidate, and a rewrite of an existing candidate all stay `pair_wrong_peer`, so the phone's Try again is never offered for a refusal a retry cannot fix. It stays remote-only (v1's codes are frozen and its shipped phone builds have no mapping for it), and it costs nothing — no confirm attempt, no MAC strike. Recovery is USER-INITIATED on the phone and deliberately never automatic: `begin` is unauthenticated, so a phone that silently re-begins on this code is an amplifier aimed at the public pairing host and buys nothing when the window is genuinely gone; confirm is never re-sent. The BODY is the contract rather than the status — the phone matches `{"code":"pair_unknown_candidate"}` and never reads the `400`, because a public edge can emit a 4xx of its own and this is the one failure whose message tells a user their code is still good, so an edge must not be able to talk them into resubmitting into a window that is not there; a body the phone cannot parse degrades to a generic "nothing was stored", which is why the shape is pinned to exactly one `code` field with `cache-control: no-store` on both paths. `(PAIR-CODE-ASCII)` asserts the minted code is exactly 8 ASCII digits before it reaches `srpComputeX`, whose password encoding is ASCII: a non-ASCII digit would silently become a verifier no phone could ever match while the user typed the right digits (the companion hit exactly this — `Char.isDigit` accepts Arabic-Indic and fullwidth digits its ASCII encoder then destroys). `(PAIR-TOKEN-BEFORE-PERSIST)` reads the Cloudflare Access token file BEFORE the window is consumed and the device persisted, because it is the only fallible read left in the completion path: reading it afterwards turned a missing or unreadable token file into the worst outcome the protocol has — the phone told `500 unknown` and honestly reporting that nothing was stored, while the desktop kept a device row nothing would ever use behind a window nothing could reopen. Failing first changes nothing at all, and the user finishes on the same still-live code once the file is fixed; everything after the persist is in-process deterministic crypto, so no post-persist failure remains that is not a programming bug. EVERY answer this host gives — success, refusal, and a body refused for size or syntax before parsing — is the pairing shape (`{code}` from a closed set) with `cache-control: no-store`; the shared bounded reader's sealed-protocol errors (`body_too_large`, `envelope_invalid`, `serverTimeMs`) are re-stated in that shape rather than escaping onto an unauthenticated host. The one-window slot is RESERVED before either opener awaits