14	## Features
15	
16	| Feature | What it is | Marker |
17	|---|---|---|
18	| Native Windows ARM64 packaging | one-click NSIS installer; ARM64 node-pty/libsql/tokenizers | `npm:@lydell/node-pty`, `bun-windows-arm64` |
19	| Window controls | native `titleBarOverlay` is the sole min/max/close set on Windows | `titleBarOverlay` |
20	| Windows behaviour fixes | child-process patch, cmd.exe fallback, force-foreground, watchdog, WebGL recovery, fast startup | `windows-child-process` |
21	| Windows user-environment merge | on Windows, `HKCU\Environment` is read through PowerShell as structured JSON and merged UNDER `process.env` at the top of the Electron main boot and BOTH host-service entries, and it replaces the login-shell snapshot entirely on win32. Upstream's snapshot spawns `%COMSPEC%` (cmd.exe) with POSIX login-shell flags (`-i -l -c`) and a POSIX body (`echo -n`, `command env`), so on EVERY Windows boot cmd printed its banner, exited 0, and the parse threw `delimiter not found` — the app then ran on whatever env block its parent held. An installer-driven relaunch missing `SUPERSET_COMPANION_BRIDGE=1` (correct in the registry the whole time) silently gated the companion bridge off and the phone reported "Superset isn't running". The reader is PowerShell rather than `reg query` because reg.exe's console text is unparseable in principle, not merely awkward: it transcodes to the OEM codepage with best-fit substitution before the bytes reach the pipe (`™`→`T`, so a non-ASCII username corrupts JAVA_HOME), a REG_SZ containing newlines is byte-identical to two separate values (a pasted blob whose second line reads `    NODE_OPTIONS    REG_SZ    --require C:\evil.js` becomes a REAL NODE_OPTIONS in the Electron main env, spread to the host-service child), and a value may itself contain the four-space field separator. The script reads the key directly and filters on `GetValueKind` — `GetEnvironmentVariables('User')` is unusable because it renders REG_MULTI_SZ as the literal `System.String[]` and pre-expands REG_EXPAND_SZ, which would wrongly expand a literal `pa%TEMP%ss` stored as REG_SZ. REG_SZ comes back verbatim and REG_EXPAND_SZ raw (`DoNotExpandEnvironmentNames`), so expansion happens in a second TS pass against the merged target and a same-hive `%ZBASE%` resolves whatever order the registry enumerated it in. process.env always wins; names are preserved byte-for-byte (a registry name with a leading space stays a different variable and cannot open the bridge); names are case-folded as Windows does; the read is memoized per process; `SystemRoot` unset fails loud rather than resolving powershell.exe through PATH; a read failure is `console.error`-loud and never fatal; values are NEVER logged (this key holds users' API keys, pinned by a tripwire test) | `(WIN-USER-ENV)` |
