| Egress fence (log-only) | a fork-owned `onBeforeRequest` observer on `session.fromPartition("persist:superset")`, installed in the boot path immediately after `app.whenReady()` and BEFORE any window exists — `MainWindow()` calls `assertEgressFenceInstalled()` and THROWS if it did not run, so the ordering is a runtime proof rather than a comment asking the next person to preserve it. It CANCELS NOTHING by design: this phase exists to prove the allowlist is complete before a later phase starts blocking. It logs ORIGIN ONLY (`scheme://host:port`) plus method, resourceType, initiator class and a first-seen timestamp with a count — never a full URL, path, query or fragment, because the companion pairing secret travels in a URL FRAGMENT and other URLs carry bearer tokens; an unparseable URL is recorded as `<unparseable>` rather than echoed. Browser-pane webviews are EXEMPT: `persist:superset` is also the `<webview>` partition (`usePersistentWebview.ts`, `browserRuntimeRegistry.ts`), so arbitrary user browsing arrives on the same listener and is counted only as a bare total — logging those origins would make this a browsing-history file and drown the app's own egress. The exemption is an ALLOWLIST, not a denylist: an origin is logged ONLY for a request positively attributed to a non-webview webContents, and everything unattributable is counted-and-dropped under a second bare total. That is not pedantry — a site in a browser pane that registers a SERVICE WORKER (any PWA) fetches on this partition with NO webContentsId at all, and a webview's in-flight requests can complete after it is destroyed; both would otherwise be classed "unknown" and have their origins logged, leaking exactly the browsing the exemption protects and able to flood the entry cap with it. The accepted cost, stated plainly: unattributable APP traffic (a main-process `net.fetch` on this session) is invisible here too, and the counter is the honest measure of what went unseen. Initiator is resolved via `w