This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.

Summary:
1. Primary Request and Intent:

   Initial investigation:
   - The user asked whether Superset inserts skill files into Claude Code and Codex instances.
   - Investigation found:
     - Upstream Superset copied bundled base skills into user-level Claude and shared agent folders.
     - This fork also creates profile junctions that expose user-owned skills inside workspace Claude profiles.

   Skill removal:
   - The user said:
     - “Remove them. I don't want any superset base skills ever injected or in any of my folders/files. remove the code that does anything like that from superset root and stem”
   - The user selected the complete “Skills everywhere” removal:
     - Delete bundled Superset base skills.
     - Delete skill-backed commands.
     - Remove packaging, installation, editing, toggling, and UI code.
     - Delete existing Superset-managed skill copies from the machine.
     - Preserve hooks, status dots, account profiles, MCP support, and user-owned skills.

   Hook ownership:
   - The user asked:
     - “the hooks etc is my own code right? - this superset skills is from upstream or are there any hooks from upstream too?”
   - Investigation established:
     - Bundled skills and their injector came from upstream.
     - Generic hook plumbing had upstream origins.
     - This fork owns the status-dot system, `superset-notify.py`, JSONL watcher, auto-resume, companion alerts, PowerShell integration, and per-workspace Claude account profiles.
     - Removing bundled skills did not require removing those fork-owned features.

   Agent policy:
   - The user initially allowed Opus agents:
     - “you can use opus sub agents if you'd like to parallelise and advice”
   - The user then explicitly changed the policy:
     - “No more Opus Agents from now on. Stick to Sol Agents, please.”
   - This remains in force verbatim.
   - All agents launched after that instruction were Sol agents or Sol code reviewers.

   Installation and delivery:
   - The user asked:
     - “can you install it? new version?”
   - The user corrected the assumption that unrelated work was unfinished:
     - “investigate - i know of no unfinished work....”
   - Investigation confirmed:
     - The unrelated workspace-exit, companion, account, and native packaging work predated the installed installer.
     - Only the skill-removal work was newer than the installed build at that point.
   - The user required:
     - “Make sure everything is committed and pushed up to main master origin when work is completed”
   - The user instructed:
     - “move these artefacts into tmp... the right place for thoraway”
   - The user selected:
     - Silent installation.
     - Preserve existing app data.
     - Reopen Superset after installation.
     - Fix both known workspace lifecycle defects before installation.
     - Fix any additional Sol-verified blocker or major defects in the active workstreams.

   Workspace lifecycle defects:
   - The user selected “Fix both first” for:
     - Automatic Snooze return cancelling pending host cleanup.
     - Kanban mismatch repair bypassing the full completion and uncompletion lifecycle.
   - The user also authorized fixing any additional Sol-verified blocker or major defects.
   - This authorization covered the durable-write defect and later lifecycle races without requiring a new UX decision or schema change.

   Overnight mode:
   - The user invoked `/mk-going-to-sleep`.
   - Sleep mode remains active.
   - No `AskUser` calls are allowed while sleep mode is active.
   - No production data or production schema changes are allowed.
   - Any unforeseen UX or app-behaviour judgement must be logged in:
     - `C:\Users\khair\Code Projects\fork-superset-windows-arm64\tmp\sleep-judgements-2026-08-31.md`
   - The log must never be committed.
   - On the next genuine wake message, every unreviewed judgement must be presented through `AskUser` before handling the new request.
   - System reminders, task notifications, automated “no visible output” prompts, and single-period keep-alives do not count as the user waking.
   - The log still contains no judgement entries.
   - Its status remains:
     - `STATUS: SLEEP MODE ACTIVE - UNREVIEWED`

   Continuing constraints:
   - Schema changes always require explicit user permission.
   - No schema shape was changed.
   - Never force-push.
   - Run automated unit tests only once, at the end, immediately before the final build/push.
   - Fetch and compare with `origin` again before final conclusions and pushing.
   - Invoke `/simplify` fully before committing, pushing, or reporting production code complete.
   - Keep Entire running.
   - Temporary scripts, logs, generated downloads, and local verification artifacts belong under `tmp` and must not be committed.
